\section{Introduction}\label{sec:compiler-gen-intro}

A \emph{compiler generator} is a program that takes a language definition (typically an interpreter or a formal semantics) and produces a compiler for that language. This idea---that compilers can be \emph{generated} rather than \emph{hand-crafted}---is one of the most ambitious in programming language research. If successful, it would dramatically reduce the cost of language implementation and ensure that compilers are correct by construction.

The theoretical foundation for compiler generators was laid by the Futamura projections (Chapter~\ref{ch:futamura}), which showed that partial evaluation of an interpreter yields a compiler, and self-application of a partial evaluator yields a compiler generator. This chapter explores the practical realization of these ideas: from the specializer approach to grammar-based compiler generation, from language workbenches to modern tools that automate significant portions of the compiler construction process.

\section{From Interpreter to Compiler}\label{sec:interp-to-compiler}

\subsection{The Fundamental Insight}

The central insight of compiler generation is that an interpreter and a compiler compute the same function---they differ only in \emph{when} the computation happens. An interpreter computes the result directly; a compiler produces code that will compute the result later. If we can systematically transform the interpreter's structure into code that defers computation, we have a compiler.

\begin{definition}[Interpreter-to-Compiler Transformation]\label{def:interp-to-compiler}
Given an interpreter $\mathit{interp} : \mathit{source} \times \mathit{input} \to \mathit{output}$, an \emph{interpreter-to-compiler transformation} produces a compiler $\mathit{comp} : \mathit{source} \to (\mathit{input} \to \mathit{output})$ such that:
\[
\mathit{comp}(s)(d) = \mathit{interp}(s, d) \quad \text{for all } s, d
\]
\end{definition}

The transformation must satisfy two criteria:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Correctness}: The compiled code must produce the same results as the interpreter.
  \item \textbf{Efficiency}: The compiled code should be significantly faster than interpretation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Manual Transformation: A Case Study}

Consider a simple calculator language with the following interpreter:

\begin{verbatim}
  let rec interp = function
    | Num n -> n
    | Add (e1, e2) -> interp e1 + interp e2
    | Mul (e1, e2) -> interp e1 * interp e2
    | Var x -> lookup x env
\end{verbatim}

A manual transformation to a compiler yields:

\begin{verbatim}
  type instruction = Push of int | Load of string | Add | Mul

  let rec compile = function
    | Num n -> [Push n]
    | Add (e1, e2) -> compile e1 @ compile e2 @ [Add]
    | Mul (e1, e2) -> compile e1 @ compile e2 @ [Mul]
    | Var x -> [Load x]
\end{verbatim}

The transformation replaces direct computation with code generation: instead of computing $\mathit{interp}(e_1) + \mathit{interp}(e_2)$, the compiler emits instructions that will perform the addition at run time.

\subsection{Systematic Transformation}

The manual transformation above follows a systematic pattern that can be automated:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Replace values with code}: Where the interpreter computes a value, the compiler emits code to compute that value.
  \item \textbf{Replace evaluation with emission}: Where the interpreter evaluates an expression, the compiler emits instructions for that expression.
  \item \textbf{Replace environments with code}: Where the interpreter looks up a variable, the compiler emits a load instruction.
  \item \textbf{Preserve structure}: The recursive structure of the interpreter is preserved in the compiler.
\end{enumerate}

This systematic transformation is exactly what partial evaluation automates (Chapter~\ref{ch:futamura}).

\section{The Specializer Approach}\label{sec:specializer-approach}

\subsection{Partial Evaluation as Compiler Generation}

The most direct approach to compiler generation is to use a partial evaluator. Given an interpreter $\mathit{interp}$, the partial evaluator $\mathit{spec}$ produces:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{comp} = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{interp})
\end{equation}

This is the second Futamura projection (Theorem~\ref{thm:second-futamura}). The resulting compiler $\mathit{comp}$ takes a source program $s$ and produces target code $\mathit{comp}(s)$.

\subsection{Requirements for the Specializer}

For the specializer approach to produce a practical compiler, the partial evaluator must satisfy several requirements:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Sufficient power}: The partial evaluator must be able to perform constant propagation, dead code elimination, loop unrolling, and function inlining.
  \item \textbf{Termination}: The partial evaluator must terminate on all interpreter inputs, including the interpreter itself (for self-application).
  \item \textbf{Efficiency}: The generated compiler must produce efficient target code. The partial evaluator must perform enough optimization to eliminate interpretation overhead.
  \item \textbf{Target language support}: The partial evaluator must be able to generate code in the target language (machine code, bytecode, or a lower-level source language).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Mix Approach to Compiler Generation}

The Mix system~\cite{jones1993partial} was the first to achieve practical compiler generation via partial evaluation. The approach involved several key innovations:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Binding-time improvement}: The interpreter is manually annotated to guide the partial evaluator. These annotations indicate which computations should be performed at compile time and which at run time.
  \item \textbf{Lambda-lifting}: Higher-order functions in the interpreter are transformed into first-order form, simplifying the partial evaluator's task.
  \item \textbf{Deforestation}: Intermediate data structures created by the interpreter are eliminated in the compiled code.
  \item \textbf{Self-application}: The partial evaluator is applied to itself to produce a compiler generator.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{tomealgorithm}[H]
\caption{Compiler Generation via Partial Evaluation}
\label{alg:compiler-gen}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \Comment{Step 1: Write an interpreter for language $L$}
\State $\mathit{interp} \gets \Call{WriteInterpreter}{L}$
\State \Comment{Step 2: Annotate the interpreter for binding-time improvement}
\State $\mathit{interp}_{\mathit{ann}} \gets \Call{Annotate}{\mathit{interp}}$
\State \Comment{Step 3: Partial evaluate the partial evaluator with the annotated interpreter}
\State $\mathit{compiler} \gets \mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{interp}_{\mathit{ann}})$
\State \Comment{Step 4: Use the compiler to compile source programs}
\State $\mathit{target} \gets \mathit{compiler}(s)$
\State \Comment{Step 5 (optional): Self-apply to get a compiler generator}
\State $\mathit{cogen} \gets \mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{spec})$
\State $\mathit{compiler}' \gets \mathit{cogen}(\mathit{interp}_{\mathit{ann}})$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{tomealgorithm}

\subsection{Limitations of the Specializer Approach}

Despite its elegance, the specializer approach has significant limitations:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Binding-time improvement is manual}: The interpreter must be carefully annotated, requiring deep understanding of both the interpreter and the partial evaluator.
  \item \textbf{Generated compilers may be inefficient}: The partial evaluator performs general-purpose specialization, which may not exploit domain-specific optimization opportunities.
  \item \textbf{Self-application is fragile}: The self-application of the partial evaluator requires careful engineering and is sensitive to the details of the implementation.
  \item \textbf{Limited optimization}: The partial evaluator performs only the optimizations it was designed for; it cannot discover new optimization opportunities specific to the source language.
\end{enumerate}

