The grammar-based approach automates the \emph{front end} of the compiler (lexical analysis, parsing, AST construction) but typically leaves the \emph{back end} (semantic analysis, optimization, code generation) to the language developer. This is because the front end is largely determined by the grammar, while the back end depends on the language's semantics and target platform.

\section{Grammar Composition}\label{sec:grammar-composition}

\subsection{The Problem of Grammar Modularity}

Traditional grammar formalisms (CFGs, EBNF) are \emph{monolithic}: a grammar is a single, self-contained specification. This makes it difficult to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Extend a language with new constructs.
  \item Combine multiple languages (e.g., embedding SQL in a host language).
  \item Reuse grammar components across languages.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Composable Grammars}

\emph{Grammar composition} allows grammars to be built from smaller, reusable components:

\begin{definition}[Grammar Composition]\label{def:grammar-composition}
A \emph{composable grammar} is a grammar defined as a set of \emph{grammar modules} $G_1, G_2, \ldots, G_n$ that can be combined using composition operators:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Union}: $G_1 \cup G_2$ combines the productions of $G_1$ and $G_2$.
  \item \textbf{Extension}: $G_1 \oplus G_2$ extends $G_1$ with new productions from $G_2$, possibly overriding existing ones.
  \item \textbf{Restriction}: $G_1 \setminus G_2$ removes the productions of $G_2$ from $G_1$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The SDF (Syntax Definition Formalism) used by Spoofax and ASF+SDF supports grammar composition natively. This enables a modular approach to language design where language constructs are defined independently and composed to form the complete language.

\subsection{Grammar Composition and Ambiguity}

A major challenge in grammar composition is \emph{ambiguity}: when two grammar modules are combined, the resulting grammar may be ambiguous even if the individual modules are not. Modern grammar composition frameworks handle this by:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Generalized parsing}: Using GLR or GLL parsers that can handle ambiguous grammars (Chapter~\ref{ch:glr-gll}).
  \item \textbf{Disambiguation filters}: Applying priority and associativity rules to select the intended parse.
  \item \textbf{Grammar analysis}: Statically checking that the composed grammar is unambiguous.
\end{enumerate}

\section{DSL Implementation Strategies}\label{sec:dsl-implementation}

\subsection{Internal vs.\ External DSLs}

\begin{definition}[Internal vs.\ External DSL]\label{def:internal-external-dsl}
\begin{itemize}
  \item An \emph{internal DSL} (embedded DSL) is implemented within a host language, using the host language's syntax and semantics. Example: LINQ in C\#, or the Racket DSL system.
  \item An \emph{external DSL} has its own syntax, parser, and tooling, independent of any host language. Example: SQL, regular expressions, Makefiles.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Internal vs.\ External DSLs}
\label{tab:internal-external-dsl}
\begin{tabular}{lp{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Internal DSL} & \textbf{External DSL} \\
\hline
Syntax & Constrained by host language & Fully customizable \\
Tooling & Inherits host language tools & Requires custom tools \\
Integration & Seamless with host language & Requires interop mechanism \\
Learning curve & Users must know host language & Users learn only the DSL \\
Implementation effort & Low (leverages host) & High (parser, tools, etc.) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Implementation Strategies for DSLs}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Interpretation}: Write an interpreter for the DSL. Simple but slow. Suitable for configuration languages and low-performance DSLs.
  \item \textbf{Translation (transpilation)}: Translate the DSL to a host language. The host language compiler handles optimization and code generation. Example: CoffeeScript to JavaScript.
  \item \textbf{Embedding}: Embed the DSL in a host language using metaprogramming facilities (macros, templates, type-level programming). The host compiler handles compilation.
  \item \textbf{Language workbench}: Use a language workbench to generate a complete tool chain. Maximum flexibility but highest implementation effort.
  \item \textbf{Partial evaluation}: Write an interpreter for the DSL and use partial evaluation to generate compiled code (the first Futamura projection).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Practical Considerations}\label{sec:compiler-gen-practical}

\subsection{When to Use Compiler Generation}

Compiler generation is most appropriate when:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Many similar languages need to be implemented (e.g., a family of DSLs).
  \item The language is simple enough that a generated compiler is competitive with a hand-crafted one.
  \item Rapid prototyping is more important than maximum performance.
  \item The language design is evolving and frequent changes are expected.
\end{itemize}

Compiler generation is less appropriate when:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Maximum performance is required (hand-tuned compilers are still faster).
  \item The language has complex semantics that are difficult to express in the generator's formalism.
  \item The target platform has unusual requirements (embedded systems, GPUs).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The State of the Art}

Modern compiler generation tools have made significant progress:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{ANTLR}~\cite{parr2013definitive}: Generates parsers from grammars, with support for multiple target languages. Widely used in industry.
  \item \textbf{LLVM}: While not a compiler generator per se, LLVM's modular architecture allows compiler front ends to be generated from language specifications.
  \item \textbf{Truffle/GraalVM}: A framework for implementing language interpreters that automatically generates JIT compilers via partial evaluation~\cite{grimmer2015truffle}.
  \item \textbf{RPython/PyPy}: A restricted subset of Python that can be translated to C. The PyPy Python interpreter is written in RPython and generates a JIT-compiled Python implementation.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The dream of automatic compiler generation dates back to the earliest days of computing. Backus's 1978 Turing Award lecture~\cite{backus1978can} argued that the conventional von Neumann style of programming was a barrier to productivity and advocated for \emph{program transformation systems} that could generate efficient code from high-level specifications. While the full vision has not been realized, modern tools have achieved significant portions of it.
\end{historical}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:compiler-gen-summary}

Compiler generators represent the convergence of several threads in programming language research: partial evaluation, grammar-based language definition, language workbenches, and domain-specific language engineering. The key achievements are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The theoretical foundation (Futamura projections) shows that compilers can be generated from interpreters by partial evaluation.
  \item Practical systems (Mix, Cogen, Truffle/GraalVM) demonstrate that generated compilers can be competitive with hand-crafted ones.
  \item Language workbenches (MPS, Xtext, Spoofax) provide comprehensive environments for language development.
  \item Grammar composition enables modular, reusable language definitions.
\end{enumerate}

The future of compiler generation lies in the integration of these approaches: language workbenches that use partial evaluation to generate efficient compilers from semantic specifications, with grammar composition for modularity and projectional editing for a seamless development experience.

\section{Advanced Topics in Compiler Generation}\label{sec:compiler-gen-advanced}

\subsection{Attribute Grammar-Based Compiler Generation}

\emph{Attribute grammars} (Chapter~\ref{ch:attribute-grammars}) provide a declarative framework for specifying both the syntax and semantics of a language. Compiler generation from attribute grammars proceeds in two steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Semantic evaluation}: The attribute grammar defines \emph{synthesized} and \emph{inherited} attributes for each grammar production. These attributes encode type checking, name resolution, and other semantic analyses.
  \item \textbf{Code generation}: Additional attributes encode code generation. The compiler generator produces code that evaluates the attributes in the correct order (topological sort of the attribute dependency graph).
\end{enumerate}

The LRC (Referenced Environment Compiler) system and the JastAdd framework demonstrate this approach: the language designer specifies the language as an attribute grammar, and the system generates a compiler.

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Generating an Evaluator Schedule for One Production}

An attribute grammar can state equations in any order. A generator must
schedule those equations before it emits a visitor, and should explain
why a schedule is unavailable when dependencies form a cycle. A
single-production scheduler illustrates the local problem without
assuming that arbitrarily recursive grammars can be checked one
production at a time.

\textbf{Contract.} Given one production with a finite list of
attribute equations, return an order for its computed attributes or
return the blocked set. Each computed attribute has one defining
equation. Every other referenced attribute is available before entry.
Assume the equations are pure and total, and store each prerequisite
only once, even if its value is used several times in the equation.

\begin{sudocode}[Plan an evaluator's local equations]
\Let{G}{\Call{ComputedDependencyGraph}{\mathrm{equations}}}
\Let{N}{\Call{Indegrees}{G}}
\Let{Q}{\text{queue of nodes with indegree zero}}
\Let{L}{\text{empty evaluation order}}
\To{Inherited inputs available on entry need no equation.}
\While{$Q$ is nonempty}
  \Let{a}{\Call{Pop}{Q}}
  \State append $a$ to $L$
  \For{each dependent $z$ of $a$ in $G$}
    \Let{N[z]}{N[z]-1}
    \If{$N[z]=0$}
      \State enqueue $z$ in $Q$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\To{Blocked nodes may include descendants of a cycle.}
\If{$|L|<|G.\mathrm{nodes}|$}
  \State \Return blocked nodes outside $L$
\EndIf
\State \Return $L$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} A sequence production has inherited position
\(p=10\) and an available left width of \(3\). Its equations
are \(r=p+3\) for the right start, \(e=r+4\) for the right
end, and \(w=e-p\) for the parent width. The generator emits
the schedule \(r,e,w\), which computes \(13,17,7\).
An equation \(r=e-4\) in place of the first would block all
three; the cycle is \(r,e\), not \(w\).

At each insertion, every computed prerequisite is already in the
schedule. Counting only computed-to-computed dependencies gives
linear work in equations and dependency edges. A generator can
report an actual cycle by finding a strongly connected component
inside the blocked graph. In a multi-production grammar, check
interactions between child attributes and parent equations as well;
local checks that assume unavailable child results are already
supplied would be unsound.

\subsection{The Role of Program Transformation}

Compiler generation can be viewed as a special case of \emph{program transformation}: the transformation takes an interpreter and produces a compiler. Program transformation systems provide the infrastructure for this:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stratego}: A language for program transformation based on \emph{strategic rewriting}. Transformations are expressed as strategies that combine rewrite rules with control flow (sequencing, choice, repetition).
  \item \textbf{TXL}: A language designed for rapid prototyping of program transformations. Supports grammar-based source representation and rule-based transformation.
  \item \textbf{DMS} (Domain Mapping System): A commercial program transformation system that supports multiple languages and provides a comprehensive infrastructure for analysis and transformation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Generating Compilers from Formal Semantics}

The most ambitious approach to compiler generation starts from a \emph{formal semantics} of the language---not an interpreter written in a programming language, but a mathematical specification of the language's meaning.

\begin{example}[From Formal Semantics to Compiler]\label{ex:semantics-to-compiler}
Given a formal semantics of a language $L$ in the form of inference rules:
\begin{semrules}{Semantics of Expressions}
  \semruleaxiom{\SemBrack{n} = n}{\text{for integer literals}} \\[4pt]
  \semrule{\SemBrack{e_1} = v_1 \quad \SemBrack{e_2} = v_2}{\SemBrack{e_1 + e_2} = v_1 + v_2}{\text{for addition}} \\[4pt]
  \semrule{\SemBrack{e} = v}{\SemBrack{\mathbf{let}\;x = e_1\;\mathbf{in}\;e_2} = \SemBrack{e_2}[v/x]}{\text{for let}}
\end{semrules}
A compiler generator can transform these rules into code generation rules:
\begin{semrules}{Code Generation}
  \semruleaxiom{\mathit{gen}(n) = [\mathit{Push}\;n]}{\text{for integer literals}} \\[4pt]
  \semrule{\mathit{gen}(e_1) = c_1 \quad \mathit{gen}(e_2) = c_2}{\mathit{gen}(e_1 + e_2) = c_1 \cdot c_2 \cdot [\mathit{Add}]}{\text{for addition}} \\[4pt]
  \semrule{\mathit{gen}(e_1) = c_1 \quad \mathit{gen}(e_2) = c_2}{\mathit{gen}(\mathbf{let}\;x = e_1\;\mathbf{in}\;e_2) = c_1 \cdot [\mathit{Store}\;x] \cdot c_2}{\text{for let}}
\end{semrules}
This transformation is systematic: the structure of the semantic rules is preserved in the code generation rules.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Deforestation Approach to Compiler Generation}

Wadler's \emph{deforestation} technique eliminates intermediate data structures in functional programs~\cite{wadler1990deforestation}. Applied to compiler generation, deforestation can eliminate the AST:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The parser produces an AST (intermediate data structure).
  \item The semantic analyzer transforms the AST (intermediate data structure).
  \item The code generator produces target code from the AST.
\end{enumerate}

Deforestation can fuse the parser and code generator, eliminating the AST entirely. The result is a \emph{one-pass compiler} that reads source code and emits target code without constructing any intermediate representation.

This approach is limited: not all compilers can be deforested (some analyses require multiple passes over the AST). But for simple languages, deforestation can produce remarkably efficient compilers.

